{
  "name": "What to Do When Your Kitchen Door Frame Is Cracked",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Finding a cracked or split kitchen door frame can be a real headache, right? It's more than just an eyesore; it can actually affect how your door functions. First, you'll want to figure out if it's a surface crack or something deeper. Small, hairline cracks might be fixable with wood filler and a good sanding, followed by priming and painting. But if you've got a larger split or a loose section, you might need to carefully remove any existing trim or door stops to get a better look at the damage. Sometimes, a strong wood adhesive and clamps can do the trick for rejoining split wood. For more significant damage, especially if the structural integrity is compromised, you might be looking at replacing a section of the jamb. Don't forget to check if the door still closes and latches properly after any repairs. If it's still sticky or misaligned, you'll need to adjust the strike plate or hinges. Getting this right ensures your door works smoothly and looks great. If you're unsure about the extent of the damage or the best repair method, it's always smart to call in an expert.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
